Study 28:                                                                                                               
Title: "Evaluation of the Biostar Chlamydia OIA assay with specimens from women attending a sexually transmitted disease
clinic"                                                                                                                 
Abstract: Chlamydia trachomatis infections are the most prevalent sexually transmitted diseases (STDs) in the United    
States... The goal of this study was to compare the performance of the Biostar Chlamydia OIA for the detection of C.    
trachomatis with the performance of cell culture, direct fluorescent-antibody (DFA) assay (Syva MicroTrak; Syva Co.,    
Palo Alto, Calif.), and PCR (Roche Amplicor Chlamydia trachomatis; Roche, Branchburg, N.J.) for the detection of C.     
trachomatis infections in women attending an urban STD clinic.                                                          
                                                                                                                        
Does it meet criteria?                                                                                                  
- Diagnostic accuracy study? Yes, evaluating Biostar Chlamydia OIA vs cell culture, DFA, and PCR                        
- Population: women attending an urban STD clinic - reproductive age                                                    
- Prospective enrollment? Not explicitly stated, but clinic-based evaluation                                            
- Without previous diagnostic testing, co-infections or complications? Not explicitly stated                            
- Consecutive or random sampling? Not explicitly stated                                                                 
- All participants received index test and reference standard? It says: "For calculations of relative test performance  
(sensitivity, specificity, and positive and negative predictive values), patient specimens that yielded positive results
by two or more of the four assays (cell culture, DFA assay, PCR, and OIA) were classified as "true infections." By these
criteria, 42 of 306 total specimens were classified as positive for C. trachomatis (positive prevalence, 13.7%), 11     
(3.6%; 10 by PCR and 1 by DFA assay) were positive by a single assay, and 253 (82.7%) were negative by all four tests." 
This suggests they tested all specimens with multiple tests, but the reference standard appears to be a composite       
(positive by 2+ tests). Let me read more carefully.                                                                     
"All culture-positive specimens were also positive by at least one other assay. Among the culture-negative specimens,   
14(5%) specimens were positive by two of the three non-culture-based assays used. By using the criterion that positivity
by at least two of the tests indicated a true infection,the relative sensitivities were as follows: culture and PCR,    
92.9% each; Biostar Chlamydia OIA, 73.8%; and DFA assay, 59.5%."                                                        
This suggests they used a composite reference standard (positivity by 2+ of the 4 tests), and then calculated           
sensitivity of each test against that composite standard.                                                               
But did all participants get all tests? It seems like they tested all specimens with all four assays: "patient specimens
that yielded positive results by two or more of the four assays"                                                        
So yes, it appears all specimens were tested with all four assays (cell culture, DFA, PCR, OIA).                        
- Presents 2x2 data? Yes, sensitivities are reported                                                                    
- Case-control? No, it appears to be evaluating specimens from clinic attendees                                         
                                                                                                                        
Let me check: "The goal of this study was to compare the performance of the Biostar Chlamydia OIA for the detection of  
C. trachomatis with the performance of cell culture, direct fluorescent-antibody (DFA) assay... and PCR... for the      
detection of C. trachomatis infections in women attending an urban STD clinic."                                         
                                                                                                                        
And: "By these criteria, 42 of 306 total specimens were classified as positive for C. trachomatis"                      
                                                                                                                        
So 306 specimens from women attending STD clinic, all tested with 4 assays.                                             
                                                                                                                        
This meets criteria:                                                                                                    
- Diagnostic accuracy study: yes (comparing test performance)                                                           
- Population: women at STD clinic (reproductive age)                                                                    
- Prospective enrollment: implied by clinic attendance, though not explicitly stated                                    
- Without previous testing/complications: not stated but plausible                                                      
- Consecutive/random sampling: not explicitly stated but likely consecutive attendees                                   
- All participants received index and reference standard: all specimens tested with multiple assays; they used a        
composite reference standard (2+ tests positive)                                                                        
- Presents 2x2 data: yes, sensitivity reported                                                                          
- Not case-control: appears to be evaluating specimens from symptomatic clinic attendees                                
                                                                                                                        
Decision: INCLUDE                                                                                                       
Rationale: Evaluation of Biostar Chlamydia OIA assay with specimens from 306 women attending an urban STD clinic. All   
specimens were tested with cell culture, DFA, PCR, and OIA assays. A composite reference standard (positivity by ≥2     
tests) was used to calculate sensitivity/specificity of each test. Reports sensitivity values (culture and PCR: 92.9%   
each; Biostar OIA: 73.8%; DFA: 59.5%), indicating 2x2 data was generated. Conducted at clinical facility (urban STD     
clinic).                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                        
Study 29:                                                                                                               
Title: "Peru PREVEN Study Team. Prevalences of sexually transmitted infections in young adults and female sex workers in
Peru: a national population‐based survey"                                                                               
Abstract: BACKGROUND We assessed prevalences of seven sexually transmitted infections (STIs) in Peru, stratified by risk
behaviours, to help to define care and prevention priorities. METHODS In a 2002 household-based survey of the general   
population, we enrolled randomly selected 18-29-year-old residents of 24 cities with populations greater than 50 000    
people. We then surveyed female sex workers (FSWs) in these cities.                                                     
                                                                                                                        
Does it meet criteria?                                                                                                  
- Diagnostic accuracy study? No, it's a prevalence survey                                                               
- While it does mention testing for CT (via NAAT), it's not evaluating test accuracy - it's measuring prevalence using a
test as a tool                                                                                                          
- Not comparing a new test to a reference standard to evaluate accuracy                                                 
                                                                                                                        
Decision: EXCLUDE                                                                                                       
Rationale: This is a population-based prevalence survey of STIs including Chlamydia trachomatis, using NAAT for         
detection. It does not evaluate the accuracy of a diagnostic test against a reference standard; rather, it uses NAAT as 
a tool to measure prevalence.                                                                                           
                                                                                                                        
Study 30:                                                                                                               
Title: "Diagnostic accuracy of rapid tests for sexually transmitted infections in symptomatic women"                    
Abstract: OBJECTIVE To determine the diagnostic accuracy of tests developed for use at the point of care for Chlamydia  
trachomatis (CT), Neisseria gonorrhoeae (NG) and syphilis in women having symptoms of lower urinary tract infection.    
METHODS Cross-sectional study involving sexually active 14-49-year-old women with lower urinary tract infection symptoms
consulting during 2010 at a private health clinic and at two public hospitals in Bogotá, Colombia. Pregnant women, those
with a previous hysterectomy or those who received antibiotics during the previous 7\u2005days were excluded. Sequential
sampling was used; sample size: 1500 women. The ACON NG and CT duo test combo and the ACON individual test plates for NG
and separately for CT were used. The QuickVue Chlamydia rapid test (RT) was also used. All of them were compared with   
nucleic acid amplification methods. The SD Bioline 3.0 and ACON test for syphilis were evaluated and compared with      
serological tests. Sensitivity and specificity were estimated.                                                          
                                                                                                                        
Does it meet criteria?                                                                                                  
- Diagnostic accuracy study? Yes, explicitly states "To determine the diagnostic accuracy"                              
- Population: sexually active 14-49-year-old women with lower urinary tract infection symptoms - symptomatic women of   
reproductive age                                                                                                        
- Prospective enrollment? It's a cross-sectional study, but they consulted during 2010 - this suggests prospective      
identification of symptomatic women                                                                                     
- Without previous diagnostic testing, co-infections or complications? Excluded: "Pregnant women, those with a previous 
hysterectomy or those who received antibiotics during the previous 7 days were excluded." So they excluded some with    
previous interventions, but doesn't mention excluding previous diagnostic testing or co-infections specifically.        
However, they are studying symptomatic women with lower urinary tract infection symptoms, which could be considered a   
complication? Wait, the selection criteria says "without previous diagnostic testing, co-infections or complications" - 
but here they are specifically enrolling symptomatic women (with lower urinary tract infection symptoms), which could be
considered having a complication (symptoms). Let me check the selection criteria again.                                 
                                                                                                                        
Selection criteria: "Included trials should have prospectively enrolled participants without previous diagnostic        
testing, co‐infections or complications and consecutively or through random sampling at primary or secondary care       
facilities."                                                                                                            
                                                                                                                        
This study is enrolling symptomatic women (with lower urinary tract infection symptoms). Having symptoms could be       
considered a "complication" in the context of the selection criteria, which likely means asymptomatic infection is      
preferred or uncomplicated cases.                                                                                       
                                                                                                                        
However, the selection criteria actually says: "We included diagnostic accuracy studies of symptomatic or asymptomatic  
nonpregnant women and men reproductive age."                                                                            
                                                                                                                        
So symptomatic OR asymptomatic are both included. The exclusion is for "previous diagnostic testing, co-infections or   
complications" - where "complications" likely refers to complications of the infection (like PID, infertility, etc.),   
not the symptoms themselves.                                                                                            
                                                                                                                        
Let me read carefully: "without previous diagnostic testing, co‐infections or complications"                            
                                                                                                                        
In the context of Chlamydia screening, "complications" would likely refer to things like pelvic inflammatory disease,   
ectopic pregnancy, etc. - sequelae of untreated infection.                                                              
                                                                                                                        
Having symptoms (like discharge) is not a complication of the infection per se, but rather a manifestation of active    
infection.                                                                                                              
                                                                                                                        
The study excluded pregnant women, those with previous hysterectomy, or recent antibiotics - which are reasonable       
exclusions.                                                                                                             
                                                                                                                        
- Consecutive or random sampling? Yes: "Sequential sampling was used" - this is consecutive sampling                    
- All participants received index test and reference standard? Yes: "All of them were compared with nucleic acid        
amplification methods." And they used multiple rapid tests.                                                             
- Presents 2x2 data? Yes: "Sensitivity and specificity were estimated." And results section gives ranges.               
- Case-control? No, it's cross-sectional symptomatic population                                                         
                                                                                                                        
Decision: INCLUDE                                                                                                       
Rationale: Cross-sectional study of 1,500 symptomatic women (14-49 years) with lower urinary tract infection symptoms at
private clinic and public hospitals in Bogotá, Colombia. Evaluated diagnostic accuracy of multiple rapid tests (ACON    
duo, individual plates, QuickVue) against nucleic acid amplification methods. Used sequential (consecutive) sampling.   
Excluded pregnant women, those with previous hysterectomy, or recent antibiotics. Reports sensitivity and specificity   
ranges for CT RT (22.7%-37.7%) and specificity (99.3%-100%), indicating 2x2 data was generated. Symptomatic women are   
included per criteria ("symptomatic or asymptomatic").                                                                  
                                                                                                                        
Study 31:                                                                                                               
Title: "Comparison of the PACE 2 assay, two amplification assays, and Clearview EIA for detection of Chlamydia          
trachomatis in female endocervical and urine specimens"                                                                 
Abstract: Screening for sexually transmitted diseases (STDs) in a greater proportion of sexually active patients has    
become an accepted protocol by most health care providers. The purpose of this study was to compare the current test    
methods for detection of Chlamydia trachomatis used at the University of South Alabama, the PACE 2 assay (Gen-Probe) and
the Clearview EIA (Wampole Laboratories), with two amplification technologies, the AMP CT (Gen-Probe) and LCx (Abbott)  
assays.                                                                                                                 
                                                                                                                        
Does it meet criteria?                                                                                                  
- Diagnostic accuracy study? Yes, comparing test methods for detection of Ct                                            
- Population: female patients (787 female patients attending one of four obstetrics-gynecology clinics)                 
- Prospective enrollment? Not explicitly stated, but clinic-based                                                       
- Without previous diagnostic testing, co-infections or complications? Not stated                                       
- Consecutive or random sampling? Not stated                                                                            
- All participants received index test and reference standard? It says: "One urine and four endocervical swab specimens 
were collected in random order from 787 female patients... Eighty-seven percent of patients had no STD-related symptoms.
Patients were considered positive for C. trachomatis if three or more assays (swab and/or urine) were positive. Abbott  
and Gen-Probe confirmed discrepant results by alternate amplified assays. A total of 66 true-positive specimens were    
detected by use of the combination of endocervical swabs and urine specimens."                                          
This suggests they used a composite reference standard (3+ assays positive), and all patients got multiple specimen     
types tested with multiple assays.                                                                                      
- Presents 2x2 data? Yes: "After discrepant analysis, sensitivities for endocervical swab specimens for the EIA and the 
PACE 2, LCx, and AMP CT assays were 50, 81, 97, and 100%, respectively. Sensitivities for the LCx and AMP CT assays with
urine specimens were 98 and 81%, respectively."                                                                         
- Case-control? No                                                                                                      
                                                                                                                        
The study compares multiple tests, using a composite reference standard. It seems all participants had multiple specimen
types tested with multiple assays.                                                                                      
                                                                                                                        
Decision: INCLUDE                                                                                                       
Rationale: Comparison of PACE 2 assay, Clearview EIA, and two amplification assays (AMP CT, LCx) for detection of       
Chlamydia trachomatis in 787 female patients at OB-GYN clinics. One urine and four endocervical swab specimens collected
per patient. Used composite reference standard (positivity by ≥3 assays). Reports sensitivity/specificity for various   
test-specimen combinations (e.g., EIA: 50% for endocervical swabs; PACE 2: 81% for endocervical swabs; AMP CT: 97% for  
endocervical swabs, 98% for urine; LCx: 81% for endocervical swabs, 98% for urine), indicating 2x2 data was generated.  
Most patients (87%) asymptomatic, which is allowed per criteria.                                                        
                                                                                                                        
